---
import Presentation from "../../../components/Presentation.astro";
import Divider from "../../../components/ui/Divider.astro";
import Layout from "../../../layouts/Layout.astro";
---

<Layout>
  <div class="container my-4">
    <div class="row justify-content-center">
      <div class="col-sm-12 col-md-10">
        <a
          href="/courses/eng-design-fab/"
          class="btn btn-sm btn-outline-light mb-3"
        >
          <i class="si-arrow-left"></i> Back
        </a>

        <h1>Session 12: Functions in Sheets</h1>
        <Divider variant="8" classes="mb-5" />

        <!-- Launch / Concept -->
        <section id="launch">
          <Presentation>
            <section>
              <h2>Session 12</h2>
              <h3 class="text-sn-theme">Functions in Sheets</h3>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>Last Session's Question</h2>
              <p>
                Which of your weights' 3 trials would you most want averaged
                automatically?
              </p>
              <p class="fragment">Today, Sheets does that math for you.</p>
            </section>

            <section data-auto-animate>
              <h2>Structure First</h2>
              <p>
                Quick recap from what you already know: clearly labeled
                headers, units included right in each header, one row per
                weight.
              </p>
            </section>

            <section data-auto-animate data-auto-animate-restart>
              <h2>Structure First</h2>
              <p>
                Transfer your Session 11 data into Sheets this way before
                anything else today.
              </p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>From Manual to Automatic</h2>
              <p>
                You could add up three trials and divide by 3 yourself.
              </p>
              <p class="fragment">
                Or you could write one formula that does it &mdash; and
                keeps doing it correctly if a number changes.
              </p>
            </section>

            <section data-auto-animate>
              <h2>Meet AVERAGE</h2>
              <p><code>=AVERAGE(range)</code></p>
            </section>

            <section data-auto-animate data-auto-animate-restart>
              <h2>Meet AVERAGE</h2>
              <p>
                Select the cell where you want the result, type the formula,
                select the range of trial cells, press enter.
              </p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>Worked Example</h2>
              <p>Trials of 12, 14, and 13 in cells B2:D2:</p>
            </section>

            <section data-auto-animate>
              <h2>Worked Example</h2>
              <p><code>=AVERAGE(B2:D2)</code></p>
            </section>

            <section data-auto-animate data-auto-animate-restart>
              <h2>Worked Example</h2>
              <p><code>=AVERAGE(B2:D2) &rarr; 13</code></p>
            </section>

            <section data-auto-animate data-auto-animate-restart>
              <h2>Meet MIN and MAX</h2>
              <ul>
                <li class="fragment"><code>=MIN(range)</code> &mdash; your smallest trial</li>
                <li class="fragment"><code>=MAX(range)</code> &mdash; your largest trial</li>
              </ul>
              <p class="fragment">Together, they show the spread at a glance.</p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>Why a Formula, Not a Typed Number</h2>
              <p>
                If you catch a typo in one of your trial cells and fix it,
                a formula recalculates instantly.
              </p>
              <p class="fragment">
                A number you typed in by hand just sits there, quietly wrong.
              </p>
            </section>

            <section data-auto-animate data-auto-animate-restart>
              <h2>Two Things to Remember</h2>
              <p><b class="text-sn-theme">A formula is smarter than a number.</b></p>
              <p class="fragment">
                It reflects your actual data, even after your data changes.
              </p>
            </section>

            <section data-auto-animate>
              <h2>Two Things to Remember</h2>
              <p><b class="text-sn-theme">Reference the cells. Don't retype the values.</b></p>
              <p class="fragment">
                <code>=AVERAGE(B2:D2)</code> stays correct. Typing "13" does
                not, the moment B2:D2 changes.
              </p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>Next Time</h2>
              <p>Session 13: Conditional Formatting &amp; Data Quality</p>
              <p class="fragment">
                Now that you have averages, let's automatically flag
                anything that looks off.
              </p>
            </section>
          </Presentation>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <!-- Build -->
        <section id="build">
          <h2>Build</h2>

          <h3>Step 1: Structure Your Table</h3>
          <p>
            Transfer your Session 11 data into Sheets: clearly labeled
            headers with units included (e.g., <code>Weight (g)</code>,
            <code>Trial 1 (mm)</code>), one row per weight.
          </p>

          <div
            class="border border-secondary rounded p-4 my-3 text-center text-secondary"
            style="border-style: dashed !important;"
          >
            [GIF PLACEHOLDER &mdash; Building the Header Row]
          </div>

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Step 2: Add an Average Column</h3>
          <p>
            For each weight's row, use <code>=AVERAGE(range)</code> to
            compute the average of its 3 trials. Reference the actual trial
            cells &mdash; don't type in a number you calculated by hand.
          </p>

          <div
            class="border border-secondary rounded p-4 my-3 text-center text-secondary"
            style="border-style: dashed !important;"
          >
            [GIF PLACEHOLDER &mdash; Typing the AVERAGE Formula]
          </div>

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Step 3: Add Min and Max Columns</h3>
          <p>
            For each row, add <code>=MIN(range)</code> and
            <code>=MAX(range)</code> for the same 3 trial cells.
          </p>

          <table class="table table-dark table-sm">
            <thead>
              <tr>
                <th>Weight</th>
                <th>Trial 1</th>
                <th>Trial 2</th>
                <th>Trial 3</th>
                <th>Average</th>
                <th>Min</th>
                <th>Max</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td></td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td></td></tr>
              <tr><td></td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td></td></tr>
              <tr><td></td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td></td></tr>
              <tr><td></td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td></td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Step 4: Sanity Check</h3>
          <p>
            For every row, confirm your Average falls between your Min and
            Max. If it doesn't, one of your three formulas is referencing
            the wrong cells.
          </p>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <!-- Checkpoint -->
        <section id="checkpoint">
          <h2>Checkpoint</h2>

          <p>Before you move on, confirm all of the following:</p>

          <ul>
            <li>Average, Min, and Max are actual formulas &mdash; not typed-in numbers</li>
            <li>Average falls between Min and Max for every single row</li>
            <li>Headers still include units, from your Session 11&ndash;12 structure</li>
          </ul>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <!-- Reflection -->
        <section id="reflection">
          <h2>Reflection</h2>

          <p>Answer these on your own. Reflections are individual, even when the rest of the session was team work.</p>

          <ol>
            <li>Why might a formula-based average be more trustworthy than one you calculated by hand and typed in?</li>
            <li>Session 13 will automatically flag rows whose trials disagree with each other too much. Based on your Min/Max columns, which weight's trials do you think are least consistent?</li>
          </ol>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <!-- Submit -->
        <section id="submit">
          <h2>Submit</h2>

          <p>Submit your Sheets table with working Average, Min, and Max formulas, and your reflection.</p>

          <h1
            class="text-center mt-5 text-sn-theme border border-primary slow-flash"
          >
            Activity Complete
          </h1>
        </section>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</Layout>
